    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "Shadow memory",
      "question": "How does AddressSanitizer know an address is bad, on every single access?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "AddressSanitizer, question one. How does it know that an address is bad, on every single load and store, at only about twice the cost? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How does <em>ASan</em> check every access, at only ~2× the cost?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "AddressSanitizer keeps one shadow byte for every eight bytes of memory. New int ten allocates forty bytes: five granules, each with shadow zero, meaning all eight bytes are addressable. Around the block, the allocator adds redzones, poisoned with a negative shadow value. The compiler inserts a check before every load and store: shift the address right by three, add a fixed offset, and read that one byte. Writing element ten lands in the right redzone. Its shadow is poisoned, so the program stops with a heap buffer overflow, printing the bad access and where the block was allocated. And a partial granule, like the last five bytes of a thirteen byte buffer, gets shadow five: only its first five bytes are valid.",
